{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is running a stitch task (Task ID: 5158) from the cobbler code generation pipeline. The task requires implementing srd017-tee requirements R3.1 through R3.4 in the go-unix-utils project. These requirements cover error reporting, write failure handling, and exit status for the `tee` command:\n   - **R3.1**: Exit 0 when all output files and stdout are written successfully\n   - **R3.2**: Exit 1 when any output file cannot be opened or written; print diagnostic to stderr identifying the failed file\n   - **R3.3**: When one output file fails, continue writing to remaining output files and stdout\n   - **R3.4**: Exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., broken pipe)\n   \n   Files to modify: `cmd/tee/main.go` and `cmd/tee/tee_test.go`. Acceptance criteria require compilation, all tests passing, go vet clean, and new differential tests for R3.1-R3.4.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries (gtee)\n   - `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` for differential testing comparing stdout, stderr, exit codes\n   - `pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` for SIGPIPE handling in cmd/ utilities\n   - `io.MultiWriter` used for writing to multiple destinations simultaneously\n   - SIGPIPE/EPIPE handling nuances between GNU tee and Go\n   - The `DiffTest` struct: `{ Name, Args, Stdin, Env, WorkDir, ExitCode, Normalize, ExpectedFiles }`\n   - Non-goals: `--output-error` modes and `-p` flag are explicitly excluded\n   - Execution constitution requires: no git commands, E8 (40-line function limit), copyright headers, write-all-then-build strategy\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **docs/specs/software-requirements/srd017-tee.yaml**\n     - Source of truth for requirements R3.1-R3.4\n     - R3.1: Exit 0 on success\n     - R3.2: Exit 1 on file open/write failure, print diagnostic to stderr\n     - R3.3: Continue writing to other files when one fails\n     - R3.4: Exit 1 on stdout write error (broken pipe)\n     - Non-goals: no --output-error modes, no -p flag\n\n   - **cmd/tee/main.go** (existing, needs modification)\n     - Current implementation has SIGPIPE handler, flag parsing (-a, -i, --append, --ignore-interrupts)\n     - `openOutputs` already handles file-open errors (prints to stderr, sets exitCode=1, continues to next file) - partial R3.2/R3.3\n     - `copyStdin` uses `io.MultiWriter` which stops ALL writes when ANY writer fails - **violates R3.3**\n     - EPIPE handling: current code catches EPIPE and does NOT set exitCode=1 - **contradicts R3.4**\n     - Full existing code:\n     ```go\n     func main() {\n         sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n         appendMode, ignoreInterrupts, files := parseFlags(os.Args[1:])\n         if ignoreInterrupts {\n             signal.Ignore(os.Interrupt)\n         }\n         writers, closers, exitCode := openOutputs(files, appendMode)\n         defer closeAll(closers)\n         if err := copyStdin(writers); err != nil {\n             if !errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE) {\n                 fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"tee: %v\\n\", err)\n                 exitCode = 1\n             }\n         }\n         if exitCode != 0 {\n             os.Exit(exitCode)\n         }\n     }\n     \n     func copyStdin(writers []io.Writer) error {\n         mw := io.MultiWriter(writers...)\n         _, err := io.Copy(mw, os.Stdin)\n         return err\n     }\n     ```\n\n   - **cmd/tee/tee_test.go** (existing, needs modification)\n     - Has tests for passthrough, empty stdin, large input, single/multi file, dash file, append mode (short/long), combined flags, ignore interrupts, write order, multi-file append, file creation, truncate\n     - Missing: tests for write-error handling (read-only path), exit code on failure, continued writing when one file fails\n     - Uses `testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")` and `exec.LookPath(\"gtee\")`\n\n   - **pkg/testutils/difftest.go** (contract only, not read)\n     - `DiffTest` struct has `ExitCode int` field for verifying non-zero exits\n     - `RunDiffTests` compares stdout, stderr, and exit code between Go and reference binaries\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet - only file reads were performed before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - **Identified core problem**: `io.MultiWriter` stops writing to ALL destinations when ANY single writer returns an error. This violates R3.3 which requires continuing to write to remaining files when one fails.\n   - **Solution needed**: Replace `io.MultiWriter` with a custom resilient writer that tracks per-destination errors, continues writing to non-failed writers, and reports failures individually to stderr.\n   - **SIGPIPE/EPIPE challenge for R3.4**: `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` kills the process on SIGPIPE before EPIPE is returned from write(). GNU tee ignores SIGPIPE and handles EPIPE in its write loop. The implementation likely needs to ignore SIGPIPE (like GNU tee does) and handle EPIPE errors in the custom writer, setting exit code 1 for stdout write failures.\n   - **Current EPIPE handling is inverted**: The existing code explicitly skips setting exitCode=1 for EPIPE errors (`if !errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE)`), but R3.4 requires exit 1 on stdout write errors including EPIPE.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - First message: Massive task description providing the full project context, SRDs, architecture, constitutions, source code listings, and the specific task (#5158) to implement srd017-tee R3.1-R3.4 with detailed requirements, design decisions, acceptance criteria, and constraints.\n   - Second message: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - Instructing to provide a summary only, no tool calls.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Implement R3.1-R3.4 in `cmd/tee/main.go`:\n     - Build a resilient multi-writer that continues writing to remaining destinations when one fails (R3.3)\n     - Report per-file write errors to stderr with diagnostic messages (R3.2)\n     - Handle stdout EPIPE properly - exit 1 (R3.4)\n     - Ensure exit 0 on full success (R3.1)\n     - May need to change SIGPIPE handling (ignore SIGPIPE, handle EPIPE in write loop)\n   - Add differential test cases to `cmd/tee/tee_test.go`:\n     - Test writing to a read-only path (R3.2 - file open failure with exit code 1)\n     - Test continued writing when one of multiple files fails (R3.3)\n     - Test exit code 0 on success (R3.1)\n     - Test stdout write error handling (R3.4)\n   - Verify: `go build ./cmd/tee/`, `go test ./cmd/tee/ -count=1`, `go vet ./cmd/tee/`\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the three required files (SRD, main.go, tee_test.go) and was about to begin the implementation phase. No code changes have been made yet. The analysis of what needs to change is complete:\n   \n   The key implementation changes needed in `cmd/tee/main.go`:\n   1. Replace `io.MultiWriter` with a custom `resilientWriter` that writes to each destination independently, tracking failures per-writer\n   2. When a write to a file fails, log the error to stderr and stop writing to that file, but continue with others\n   3. Handle SIGPIPE differently - possibly ignore it and handle EPIPE in the write loop\n   4. Fix EPIPE handling to exit 1 (currently it's excluded from error reporting)\n   \n   For `cmd/tee/tee_test.go`:\n   1. Add test cases using `DiffTest` with `ExitCode: 1` for error scenarios\n   2. Test writing to non-writable paths\n   3. Test that other files still get written when one fails\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Implement the code changes to `cmd/tee/main.go` and `cmd/tee/tee_test.go` as described in the task. The task description states: \"Implement srd017-tee requirement R3.1-R3.4 as specified in the SRD\" and the design decision D1 says: \"Extend the existing cmd/tee implementation -- do not rewrite existing code\". The key change is replacing `copyStdin` with a resilient multi-writer implementation and adding corresponding differential tests. After writing all code, run `go build -o bin/tee ./cmd/tee/`, `go vet ./cmd/tee/`, and `go test ./cmd/tee/ -count=1` to verify.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5158/bd09a5a3-e61f-4fcc-856a-0c9a1fbde06a.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd09a5a3-e61f-4fcc-856a-0c9a1fbde06a","uuid":"50b050ac-e52b-46ec-8413-d2970085a710","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:18:49.768Z","isSynthetic":true}
