plementation, I'm going to refactor away from `io.MultiWriter` and instead write to each destination individually in a loop, tracking which writers have failed so I can skip them going forward.\n\nNow I'm structuring the main function to handle signal ignoring, flag parsing, and output setup, then delegating the actual copying logic to a separate function that manages errors and exit codes. I'm also defining an output struct and a copyToAll function that will handle writing to multiple destinations while tracking failures and reporting errors to stderr.\n\nDefining the output struct and copyToAll function... I need to fix the return statement in `writeAll` — it's incomplete. And I'm realizing `openOutputs` should return a slice of `namedWriter` structs instead, initializing with stdout and then appending file writers as they're opened successfully, tracking any errors that occur during file opening.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the error message format for stdout — GNU tee uses locale-dependent quoting, but since I'm focusing on testable scenarios, I'll skip the stdout EPIPE case and instead test file creation failures and mixed success/failure scenarios where some files are writable and others aren't.\n\nI need to understand what the DiffTest framework actually compares. Looking back at the code, it seems to check exit codes and stdout, but I'm not entirely sure if stderr is included in the comparison. For tests involving error messages, this matters — if stderr isn't compared, I might need to use Normalize functions or just be careful about what I'm testing. Let me move forward with the implementation and adjust based on what the tests reveal.\n\nThe core problems I'm addressing are that `io.MultiWriter` fails completely when any destination fails, EPIPE on stdout currently exits cleanly instead of reporting an error, and the current code explicitly suppresses EPIPE errors. My approach is to replace the SIGPIPE handler with signal ignoring, swap out `io.MultiWriter