{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wzgb38YQB61w7QBmzwqWuj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\t// Package testutils provides the differential testing harness for go-unix-utils.\n5\t// It executes a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and\n6\t// compares stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n7\t// Implements srd001-testutils.\n8\tpackage testutils\n9\t\n10\timport (\n11\t\t\"bytes\"\n12\t\t\"context\"\n13\t\t\"errors\"\n14\t\t\"fmt\"\n15\t\t\"os\"\n16\t\t\"os/exec\"\n17\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n18\t\t\"regexp\"\n19\t\t\"strings\"\n20\t\t\"testing\"\n21\t\t\"time\"\n22\t)\n23\t\n24\tconst defaultTimeout = 10 * time.Second\n25\t\n26\t// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n27\t// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\n28\ttype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n29\t\n30\t// DiffTest defines a single differential test case.\n31\t//\n32\t// R1.1: Name identifies the subtest (used with t.Run). Args holds command-line\n33\t// arguments passed to both binaries.\n34\t//\n35\t// R1.2: Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil\n36\t// slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is\n37\t// immediately closed. ExitCode defaults to 0 when left as the zero value.\n38\t//\n39\t// R1.3: Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n40\t// LC_ALL=C applied as a default override. When non-nil, KEY=VALUE pairs are\n41\t// merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new keys\n42\t// are added.\n43\t//\n44\t// R1.4: Normalize holds a slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout and\n45\t// stderr of both binaries before comparison. Nil or empty means no normalization.\n46\ttype DiffTest struct {\n47\t\tName          string\n48\t\tArgs          []string\n49\t\tStdin         []byte\n50\t\tEnv           []string\n51\t\tWorkDir       string\n52\t\tExitCode      int\n53\t\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n54\t\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n55\t}\n56\t\n57\tvar tsPatterns = []*regexp.Regexp{\n58\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`[A-Z][a-z]{2}\\s+\\d{1,2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n59\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n60\t\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n61\t}\n62\t\n63\t// TimestampNormalizer replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed\n64\t// placeholder string.\n65\t// R4.2: built-in normalizer for cmd/ts tests.\n66\tvar TimestampNormalizer NormalizeFunc = func(b []byte) []byte {\n67\t\tresult := b\n68\t\tfor _, pat := range tsPatterns {\n69\t\t\tresult = pat.ReplaceAll(result, []byte(\"<TIMESTAMP>\"))\n70\t\t}\n71\t\treturn result\n72\t}\n73\t\n74\ttype binaryResult struct {\n75\t\tstdout   []byte\n76\t\tstderr   []byte\n77\t\texitCode int\n78\t}\n79\t\n80\t// RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest, executing goBinary and\n81\t// refBinary with identical inputs and comparing their outputs.\n82\t// R2.1: accepts paths to Go and reference binaries plus test cases.\n83\tfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n84\t\tt.Helper()\n85\t\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n86\t\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n87\t\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n88\t\t\t})\n89\t\t}\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\tfunc runSubtest(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tc DiffTest) {\n93\t\tt.Helper()\n94\t\tworkDir := tc.WorkDir\n95\t\tif workDir == \"\" {\n96\t\t\tworkDir = t.TempDir()\n97\t\t}\n98\t\tenv := buildEnv(tc.Env)\n99\t\tgoRes := runBinary(t, goBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n100\t\trefRes := runBinary(t, refBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n101\t\tgoRes = applyNormalizers(goRes, tc.Normalize)\n102\t\trefRes = applyNormalizers(refRes, tc.Normalize)\n103\t\tcompareOutputs(t, tc, goRes, refRes)\n104\t\tcheckExpectedFiles(t, workDir, tc.ExpectedFiles)\n105\t}\n106\t\n107\tfunc runBinary(\n108\t\tt *testing.T, binary string, args []string,\n109\t\tstdin []byte, env []string, workDir string,\n110\t) binaryResult {\n111\t\tt.Helper()\n112\t\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), defaultTimeout)\n113\t\tdefer cancel()\n114\t\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, binary, args...)\n115\t\tcmd.Env = env\n116\t\tcmd.Dir = workDir\n117\t\tvar stdout, stderr bytes.Buffer\n118\t\tcmd.Stdout = &stdout\n119\t\tcmd.Stderr = &stderr\n120\t\tif stdin != nil {\n121\t\t\tcmd.Stdin = bytes.NewReader(stdin)\n122\t\t}\n123\t\terr := cmd.Run()\n124\t\tif ctx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n125\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"binary %s timed out after %v\", binary, defaultTimeout)\n126\t\t}\n127\t\treturn extractResult(t, binary, err, stdout.Bytes(), stderr.Bytes())\n128\t}\n129\t\n130\tfunc extractResult(\n131\t\tt *testing.T, binary string, err error,\n132\t\tstdout, stderr []byte,\n133\t) binaryResult {\n134\t\tt.Helper()\n135\t\tif err == nil {\n136\t\t\treturn binaryResult{stdout: stdout, stderr: stderr, exitCode: 0}\n137\t\t}\n138\t\tvar exitErr *exec.ExitError\n139\t\tif errors.As(err, &exitErr) {\n140\t\t\treturn binaryResult{\n141\t\t\t\tstdout:   stdout,\n142\t\t\t\tstderr:   stderr,\n143\t\t\t\texitCode: exitErr.ExitCode(),\n144\t\t\t}\n145\t\t}\n146\t\tt.Fatalf(\"failed to start binary %s: %v\", binary, err)\n147\t\treturn binaryResult{}\n148\t}\n149\t\n150\tfunc buildEnv(testEnv []string) []string {\n151\t\tenvMap := make(map[string]string)\n152\t\tfor _, entry := range os.Environ() {\n153\t\t\tkey, val, _ := strings.Cut(entry, \"=\")\n154\t\t\tenvMap[key] = val\n155\t\t}\n156\t\tenvMap[\"LC_ALL\"] = \"C\"\n157\t\tfor _, entry := range testEnv {\n158\t\t\tkey, val, _ := strings.Cut(entry, \"=\")\n159\t\t\tenvMap[key] = val\n160\t\t}\n161\t\tresult := make([]string, 0, len(envMap))\n162\t\tfor k, v := range envMap {\n163\t\t\tresult = append(result, k+\"=\"+v)\n164\t\t}\n165\t\treturn result\n166\t}\n167\t\n168\tfunc applyNormalizers(res binaryResult, fns []NormalizeFunc) binaryResult {\n169\t\tfor _, fn := range fns {\n170\t\t\tres.stdout = fn(res.stdout)\n171\t\t\tres.stderr = fn(res.stderr)\n172\t\t}\n173\t\treturn res\n174\t}\n175\t\n176\tfunc compareOutputs(t *testing.T, tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) {\n177\t\tt.Helper()\n178\t\tstdoutMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stdout, refRes.stdout)\n179\t\tstderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)\n180\t\texitMatch := goRes.exitCode == refRes.exitCode\n181\t\tif stdoutMatch && stderrMatch && exitMatch {\n182\t\t\treturn\n183\t\t}\n184\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s\", formatDivergence(tc, goRes, refRes))\n185\t}\n186\t\n187\tfunc formatDivergence(tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) string {\n188\t\tvar b strings.Builder\n189\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"divergence detected\\n\")\n190\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  args:        %v\\n\", tc.Args)\n191\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  stdin:       %s\\n\", formatStdin(tc.Stdin))\n192\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref stdout:  %q\\n\", refRes.stdout)\n193\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  stdout:  %q\\n\", goRes.stdout)\n194\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref stderr:  %q\\n\", refRes.stderr)\n195\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  stderr:  %q\\n\", goRes.stderr)\n196\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref exit:    %d\\n\", refRes.exitCode)\n197\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  exit:    %d\\n\", goRes.exitCode)\n198\t\treturn b.String()\n199\t}\n200\t\n201\tfunc formatStdin(stdin []byte) string {\n202\t\tif stdin == nil {\n203\t\t\treturn \"<nil>\"\n204\t\t}\n205\t\tif len(stdin) > 256 {\n206\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%q... (truncated from %d bytes)\", stdin[:256], len(stdin))\n207\t\t}\n208\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%q\", stdin)\n209\t}\n210\t\n211\tfunc checkExpectedFiles(t *testing.T, workDir string, expected map[string][]byte) {\n212\t\tt.Helper()\n213\t\tif expected == nil {\n214\t\t\treturn\n215\t\t}\n216\t\tfor relPath, want := range expected {\n217\t\t\tfullPath := filepath.Join(workDir, relPath)\n218\t\t\tgot, err := os.ReadFile(fullPath)\n219\t\t\tif err != nil {\n220\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected file %s: %v\", relPath, err)\n221\t\t\t}\n222\t\t\tif !bytes.Equal(got, want) {\n223\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"file %s divergence\\n  expected: %q\\n  actual:   %q\",\n224\t\t\t\t\trelPath, want, got)\n225\t\t\t}\n226\t\t}\n227\t}\n228\t\n229\t// ComposeNormalizers returns a single NormalizeFunc that applies the given\n230\t// functions in order.\n231\t// R4.4: convenience for combining multiple normalizers.\n232\tfunc ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc {\n233\t\treturn func(b []byte) []byte {\n234\t\t\tfor _, fn := range fns {\n235\t\t\t\tb = fn(b)\n236\t\t\t}\n237\t\t\treturn b\n238\t\t}\n239\t}\n240\t\n241\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd09a5a3-e61f-4fcc-856a-0c9a1fbde06a","uuid":"63292ef9-6bab-444c-9886-28eaa5029aa0","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:20:26.845Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5158/pkg/testutils/difftest.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Package testutils provides the differential testing harness for go-unix-utils.\n// It executes a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and\n// compares stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n// Implements srd001-testutils.\npackage testutils\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\nconst defaultTimeout = 10 * time.Second\n\n// NormalizeFunc transforms raw output bytes before comparison.\n// R1.4: named type alias for normalization functions.\ntype NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte\n\n// DiffTest defines a single differential test case.\n//\n// R1.1: Name identifies the subtest (used with t.Run). Args holds command-line\n// arguments passed to both binaries.\n//\n// R1.2: Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil\n// slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is\n// immediately closed. ExitCode defaults to 0 when left as the zero value.\n//\n// R1.3: Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n// LC_ALL=C applied as a default override. When non-nil, KEY=VALUE pairs are\n// merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new keys\n// are added.\n//\n// R1.4: Normalize holds a slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout and\n// stderr of both binaries before comparison. Nil or empty means no normalization.\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n\nvar tsPatterns = []*regexp.Regexp{\n\tregexp.MustCompile(`[A-Z][a-z]{2}\\s+\\d{1,2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}\\s+\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n\tregexp.MustCompile(`\\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}`),\n}\n\n// TimestampNormalizer replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed\n// placeholder string.\n// R4.2: built-in normalizer for cmd/ts tests.\nvar TimestampNormalizer NormalizeFunc = func(b []byte) []byte {\n\tresult := b\n\tfor _, pat := range tsPatterns {\n\t\tresult = pat.ReplaceAll(result, []byte(\"<TIMESTAMP>\"))\n\t}\n\treturn result\n}\n\ntype binaryResult struct {\n\tstdout   []byte\n\tstderr   []byte\n\texitCode int\n}\n\n// RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest, executing goBinary and\n// refBinary with identical inputs and comparing their outputs.\n// R2.1: accepts paths to Go and reference binaries plus test cases.\nfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc runSubtest(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tc DiffTest) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tworkDir := tc.WorkDir\n\tif workDir == \"\" {\n\t\tworkDir = t.TempDir()\n\t}\n\tenv := buildEnv(tc.Env)\n\tgoRes := runBinary(t, goBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n\trefRes := runBinary(t, refBinary, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env, workDir)\n\tgoRes = applyNormalizers(goRes, tc.Normalize)\n\trefRes = applyNormalizers(refRes, tc.Normalize)\n\tcompareOutputs(t, tc, goRes, refRes)\n\tcheckExpectedFiles(t, workDir, tc.ExpectedFiles)\n}\n\nfunc runBinary(\n\tt *testing.T, binary string, args []string,\n\tstdin []byte, env []string, workDir string,\n) binaryResult {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), defaultTimeout)\n\tdefer cancel()\n\tcmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, binary, args...)\n\tcmd.Env = env\n\tcmd.Dir = workDir\n\tvar stdout, stderr bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stdout = &stdout\n\tcmd.Stderr = &stderr\n\tif stdin != nil {\n\t\tcmd.Stdin = bytes.NewReader(stdin)\n\t}\n\terr := cmd.Run()\n\tif ctx.Err() == context.DeadlineExceeded {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"binary %s timed out after %v\", binary, defaultTimeout)\n\t}\n\treturn extractResult(t, binary, err, stdout.Bytes(), stderr.Bytes())\n}\n\nfunc extractResult(\n\tt *testing.T, binary string, err error,\n\tstdout, stderr []byte,\n) binaryResult {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\treturn binaryResult{stdout: stdout, stderr: stderr, exitCode: 0}\n\t}\n\tvar exitErr *exec.ExitError\n\tif errors.As(err, &exitErr) {\n\t\treturn binaryResult{\n\t\t\tstdout:   stdout,\n\t\t\tstderr:   stderr,\n\t\t\texitCode: exitErr.ExitCode(),\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tt.Fatalf(\"failed to start binary %s: %v\", binary, err)\n\treturn binaryResult{}\n}\n\nfunc buildEnv(testEnv []string) []string {\n\tenvMap := make(map[string]string)\n\tfor _, entry := range os.Environ() {\n\t\tkey, val, _ := strings.Cut(entry, \"=\")\n\t\tenvMap[key] = val\n\t}\n\tenvMap[\"LC_ALL\"] = \"C\"\n\tfor _, entry := range testEnv {\n\t\tkey, val, _ := strings.Cut(entry, \"=\")\n\t\tenvMap[key] = val\n\t}\n\tresult := make([]string, 0, len(envMap))\n\tfor k, v := range envMap {\n\t\tresult = append(result, k+\"=\"+v)\n\t}\n\treturn result\n}\n\nfunc applyNormalizers(res binaryResult, fns []NormalizeFunc) binaryResult {\n\tfor _, fn := range fns {\n\t\tres.stdout = fn(res.stdout)\n\t\tres.stderr = fn(res.stderr)\n\t}\n\treturn res\n}\n\nfunc compareOutputs(t *testing.T, tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tstdoutMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stdout, refRes.stdout)\n\tstderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)\n\texitMatch := goRes.exitCode == refRes.exitCode\n\tif stdoutMatch && stderrMatch && exitMatch {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tt.Fatalf(\"%s\", formatDivergence(tc, goRes, refRes))\n}\n\nfunc formatDivergence(tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) string {\n\tvar b strings.Builder\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"divergence detected\\n\")\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  args:        %v\\n\", tc.Args)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  stdin:       %s\\n\", formatStdin(tc.Stdin))\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref stdout:  %q\\n\", refRes.stdout)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  stdout:  %q\\n\", goRes.stdout)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref stderr:  %q\\n\", refRes.stderr)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  stderr:  %q\\n\", goRes.stderr)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  ref exit:    %d\\n\", refRes.exitCode)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"  go  exit:    %d\\n\", goRes.exitCode)\n\treturn b.String()\n}\n\nfunc formatStdin(stdin []byte) string {\n\tif stdin == nil {\n\t\treturn \"<nil>\"\n\t}\n\tif len(stdin) > 256 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%q... (truncated from %d bytes)\", stdin[:256], len(stdin))\n\t}\n\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%q\", stdin)\n}\n\nfunc checkExpectedFiles(t *testing.T, workDir string, expected map[string][]byte) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif expected == nil {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfor relPath, want := range expected {\n\t\tfullPath := filepath.Join(workDir, relPath)\n\t\tgot, err := os.ReadFile(fullPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected file %s: %v\", relPath, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !bytes.Equal(got, want) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"file %s divergence\\n  expected: %q\\n  actual:   %q\",\n\t\t\t\trelPath, want, got)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// ComposeNormalizers returns a single NormalizeFunc that applies the given\n// functions in order.\n// R4.4: convenience for combining multiple normalizers.\nfunc ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc {\n\treturn func(b []byte) []byte {\n\t\tfor _, fn := range fns {\n\t\t\tb = fn(b)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn b\n\t}\n}\n\n","numLines":241,"startLine":1,"totalLines":241}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CZUgAFc541nxAoTxB24MMW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the full picture. `RunDiffTests` compares stdout, stderr, AND exit code between go and ref binaries. All three must match.\n\nThis means for error tests:\n- The error messages on stderr need to match between our binary and gtee\n- The exit codes need to match\n- stdout needs to match\n\nFor a write-error test (writing to a read-only path), the stderr format needs to match gtee's output. GNU tee format: `tee: FILENAME: Permission denied`. Our format: `tee: FILENAME: permission denied` (if using unwrapErr). Actually, the OS error strings are the same regardless of the program, so as long as we format `tee: NAME: ERROR` the same way, it should match.\n\n\nI need to check exactly how GNU coreutils formats file error messages—whether it includes quotes around the filename and what the exact capitalization is for the error text, since those details matter for the diff tests to pass. The key difference is that GNU coreutils uses `quotearg_colon` to add single quotes around filenames in error messages, so gtee would output `tee: 'filename': Permission denied`, while our implementation outputs without quotes. The program name in error messages comes from argv[0], so if gtee is invoked as `gtee`, it might report that name instead of `tee`, though GNU coreutils typically hardcodes the program name in the error function.\n\nLooking at the existing test structure, there aren't any tests that currently exercise the file open error path, so I need to decide how to handle stderr matching in differential tests—either normalize the program name prefix, use standalone tests instead, or just verify exit codes and stdout while accepting stderr differences.\n\nThe real constraint is that RunDiffTests creates a fresh temp directory for each test, so I'd need a way to set up readonly directories or other error conditions before the test actually runs, which might require a custom setup function rather than relying on the standard test harness. Writing the test assertions...\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's an issue with my approach—opening a read-only file with O_WRONLY will fail at the open stage, which tests the error handling I've already covered. For R3.3, I need a different strategy where one file fails to open while others succeed, so I can verify the partial writes are preserved.\n\nA better approach might be to write to a path inside a read-only directory—that way the file creation itself fails, but it's a cleaner test case than trying to simulate write errors during actual IO, which is much harder to reproduce reliably across platforms. I'm laying out the test cases I need to add—one for verifying the exit code and output when a write fails to a read-only directory while successfully writing elsewhere, and another focused on the specific requirement that tee continues despite errors. I should also verify that success cases return exit code zero, though existing tests probably cover that. Before I finalize the error message format, I'm realizing I don't actually need to match gtee's stderr exactly—I can just compare exit codes between the two implementations and then verify the go version's behavior independently. The `openOutputs` function already handles file open errors by logging them to stderr and continuing to process other files, which covers the requirements. However, write errors that occur during actual data transfer need different handling since `io.MultiWriter` will fail the entire operation if any writer fails—I need to implement per-writer error handling to match the resilient behavior of the original `tee` command.\n\nRegarding the SIGPIPE handling change, replacing `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` with `signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE)` is a necessary functional modification to meet the requirements, not a rewrite of the existing code. The key insight is that I should make minimal, targeted extensions: changing SIGPIPE handling, replacing the copy function with a resilient version, and adjusting the writer type to support error reporting—these are all extensions rather than rewrites of the existing implementation.\n\nWriting the main.go file... Writing the file I/O logic... I'm verifying that all the helper functions stay under the 40-line limit, and they do. Now I'm thinking through the error handling for write failures—when writing to stdout and getting EPIPE since SIGPIPE is ignored, `os.Stdout.Write()` returns a `*os.PathError` that wraps the syscall error, so `unwrapErr` should properly strip that wrapper to show the underlying error. we'd get `syscall.EPIPE` which would print as `broken pipe`. So the error message would be:\n```\ntee: standard output: broken pipe\n```\n\nGNU tee with LC_ALL=C would print something like:\n```\ntee: standard output: Broken pipe\n```\n\nNote the capitalization difference: \"broken pipe\" vs \"Broken pipe\". On macOS, `strerror(EPIPE)` returns \"Broken pipe\" (with capital B). In Go, `syscall.EPIPE.Error()` returns the string from `strerror()` on macOS, so it should match GNU tee's output.\n\nFor write errors, the error messages should align since both use `strerror()`. The main difference for testing would be the program name itself (tee vs gtee), which I can handle with standalone tests. I'm also wondering about the \"standard output\" string—GNU tee uses gettext translations, so in LC_ALL=C it would be \"standard output\", but I need to verify how it's actually formatted in the source.\n\nLooking at the GNU coreutils code, the error function formats as `program_name: message: strerror(errno)`, so the output would be `tee: standard output: Broken pipe`. When run as `gtee`, it would be `gtee: standard output: Broken pipe`, which won't match our output in differential tests. I'll use standalone tests for error scenarios instead.\n\nFor SIGPIPE handling, using `signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE)` prevents the process from dying and makes writes to broken pipes return EPIPE errors, which is what we need. The key difference is that `signal.Notify` would require a goroutine to handle the signal, whereas ignoring it lets the write call fail directly with an error we can catch. by InstallSIGPIPEHandler), Go's runtime is told to deliver the signal via the channel. But Go's runtime also has its own SIGPIPE handling: for writes to pipes/sockets, Go's runtime catches SIGPIPE internally and returns an EPIPE error from the write. The `signal.Notify` overrides this.\n\nActually, let me reconsider Go's SIGPIPE behavior more carefully. When SIGPIPE arrives from the kernel, Go normally raises it on the thread which terminates the program. But if I use `signal.Notify` to receive SIGPIPE, it gets delivered to the channel instead and the program doesn't exit. For pipe writes specifically, SIGPIPE gets generated on closed pipes, and by default this terminates the process unless I've set up signal handling.\n\nSo I have two options: `signal.Notify` catches SIGPIPE and runs a handler (which could exit cleanly), or `signal.Ignore` just ignores it entirely. Either way, the write returns EPIPE. I'll go with `signal.Ignore` since it's simpler and achieves the same goal of preventing the default termination. I also need to remove the `sys` import since I'm no longer using `InstallSIGPIPEHandler`.\n\nActually, wait—the CLAUDE.md guideline says all cmd utilities writing to stdout should call `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`, but the SRD for tee specifically requires exiting with code 1 on stdout write errors. That requirement takes precedence, so I'll replace the handler call with `signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE)` and clean up the import.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to test the broken stdout behavior. I can write a test that pipes stdout to a reader we close immediately, then verify the binary exits with code 1. Let me work through the test setup...\n\nWriting test code...\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's a subtlety here with how the OS handles closed stdout pipes in child processes. pipe, and the parent reads from it. If I close the read end of the pipe, the child's writes to stdout will fail with EPIPE.\n\nActually, the issue is that `cmd.Stdout = w` sets the child's stdout to the write end of the pipe. If I close the read end `r`, then the child's writes to `w` will eventually fail with EPIPE (once the pipe buffer fills or on the first write depending on the OS).\n\nBut there's a race: I need to close `r` before the child starts writing. If I close `r` immediately after creating the pipe and before `cmd.Run()`, that should work.\n\nHmm, actually there's another issue: `cmd.Run()` might fail to start because stdout is a broken pipe. Let me think...\n\nNo, `cmd.Run()` starts the process and waits for it. The process starts fine, and when it tries to write to stdout (the write end of the pipe), it will fail with EPIPE because the read end is closed.\n\nOn Unix systems, closing the read end of a pipe before writing causes the write to fail with EPIPE once the buffer fills up—the pipe has a limited capacity, typically 64KB on macOS, so sending 1MB of data should definitely trigger the error. The tricky part is that the child process inherits the write end of the pipe, and if both the parent and child have it open, I need to make sure the parent closes its copy so the child's writes actually fail. I'm trying to set up a test that triggers a broken pipe error by piping the binary's output through commands that exit early, using shell pipelines with `dd` and `head` to create the condition where writes fail.\n\nActually, I think the simpler approach is to create a pipe directly in the test—close the read end immediately so any writes to the write end will fail with EPIPE. The key insight is that after calling `cmd.Start()`, I need to close the write end in the parent process so the child's writes will actually encounter the broken pipe condition. The loop continues reading from stdin until it reaches EOF, then tee exits with code 1. After stdout fails, all writes go to failed writers, which is wasteful but matches GNU tee's behavior of consuming the entire input stream. I should just implement the straightforward approach rather than overthinking the edge cases.\n\nWriting test code... Writing the test function...\n\nI need to think through the pipe file descriptor lifecycle more carefully. When I set `cmd.Stdout = pw`, the child process inherits the write end of the pipe as its stdout file descriptor. After the child starts, both the parent and child have references to the write end—the parent through the `pw` variable and the child through its file descriptor. If I close `pw` in the parent, the child still holds the write end open via its inherited file descriptor. Only when I close `pr` does the pipe have no readers left, which causes the child's writes to fail. Writing the test function...\n\nNow I'm realizing I need to think through the pipe closure sequence more carefully. When I close the write end after starting the command, subsequent writes from the child process will fail with EPIPE, which triggers the signal handler I set up. But I should verify that stderr isn't interfering—leaving it unset means it gets discarded, which is fine for this test case.\n\nI'm also wondering about the exec package's cleanup behavior. It turns out that `cmd.Wait()` doesn't close file descriptors that were explicitly set by the user, so closing the pipe in the parent after `Start()` is safe and won't cause double-close issues.\n\nNow I need to write out the full test implementation and also add a comparison with GNU tee to verify the differential behavior.\n\nWriting the test function... I'm realizing this test approach is getting too complicated. Let me simplify and just verify the binary exits with code 1 as specified. I'll add two focused tests: one for write errors to an unwritable path and another for a broken stdout pipe, which together cover the exit code requirements. Now I'm ready to write the actual implementation.\n\nWriting main.go... Writing the file handling logic...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the core copy loop—reading from stdin in chunks and writing to all output files, tracking errors along the way. The writeAll function iterates through each output and attempts to write the data, marking any file that fails as invalid so we skip it in future writes. There's a corner case about stderr errors when printing diagnostics, but that's not worth handling for this implementation.\n\nI should also verify the exact error message format that GNU tee uses. The string \"standard output\" needs to match what the test framework expects, and the quoting style around it depends on the locale settings—in C locale it uses single quotes around the filename in the error message.\n\nLooking at our current implementation, we're using a simple format without quotes around the filename, which differs from GNU tee's approach of wrapping the name in quotes. This mismatch might cause test failures if the framework is comparing exact error message output.\n\nHowever, since the specification only requires identifying the failed file without mandating a specific format, and our error tests can't use the DiffTest framework anyway due to program name variations, I'll stick with our simpler format. Now let me move forward with the implementation, keeping in mind how we handle EPIPE errors from stdin operations.\n\nThe existing code skips EPIPE errors entirely, which violates R3.4's requirement to exit with code 1 on stdout write failures. The subtlety here is that the current SIGPIPE handler catches the signal and exits 0, so EPIPE from stdout never reaches the error handling code—the check for EPIPE is really for cases where it somehow escapes, like from file writes. My approach fixes this by ignoring SIGPIPE, letting writes return EPIPE, catching those errors in writeAll, marking stdout as failed, reporting the error, and setting the exit code appropriately.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the implementation. I need to verify the test file has the right imports—it already has `os/exec` and `testing`, so I just need to add `errors` and make sure `os` is there for the broken stdout test.\n\nWriting the implementation files...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7426,"cache_read_input_tokens":30378,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7426},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd09a5a3-e61f-4fcc-856a-0c9a1fbde06a","uuid":"8e5e48c9-b33f-44ed-ab45-cea1b3d76c75"}
